Answers:

1. If Power BI is the only frontend supporting Redshift, why not use Athena and save money?

First, clarify the incorrect assumption.

Both Amazon Redshift and Amazon Athena can connect to Microsoft Power BI.

But they serve different workloads.

Redshift
	•	Fully managed data warehouse
	•	Optimized for BI workloads
	•	Supports:
	•	materialized views
	•	stored procedures
	•	workload management
	•	row/column security
	•	high concurrency

Transformations in Redshift
	•	Complex joins
	•	Aggregations
	•	Window functions
	•	Stored procedures
	•	ELT transformations

Example:

Sales fact table joined with 10 dimension tables
Daily aggregates
Ranking customers by region

Athena

Serverless SQL engine on Amazon S3.

You pay per query scanned.

Good for:
	•	ad hoc queries
	•	data lake exploration

Limitations:
	•	slower for complex BI queries
	•	limited concurrency
	•	no native indexing
	•	weaker governance

Transformations in Athena

Mostly:
	•	simple SQL
	•	filtering
	•	basic aggregation

Example:

SELECT country, SUM(revenue)
FROM sales
GROUP BY country

Architect Answer

Use Athena for exploration, Redshift for production BI dashboards.

